{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nNeko is playing with his toys on the backyard of Aki's house. Aki decided to play a prank on him, by secretly putting catnip into Neko's toys. Unfortunately, he went overboard and put an entire bag of catnip into the toys...It took Neko an entire day to turn back to normal. Neko reported to Aki that he saw a lot of weird things, including a trie of all correct bracket sequences of length $$$2n$$$.The definition of correct bracket sequence is as follows:  The empty sequence is a correct bracket sequence,  If $$$s$$$ is a correct bracket sequence, then $$$(\\,s\\,)$$$ is a correct bracket sequence,  If $$$s$$$ and $$$t$$$ are a correct bracket sequence, then $$$st$$$ is also a correct bracket sequence. For example, the strings \"(())\", \"()()\" form a correct bracket sequence, while \")(\" and \"((\" not.Aki then came up with an interesting problem: What is the size of the maximum matching (the largest set of edges such that there are no two edges with a common vertex) in this trie? Since the answer can be quite large, print it modulo $$$10^9 + 7$$$.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.util.*;\nimport java.util.stream.IntStream;\n\npublic class Animal {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = scanner.nextInt();\n        long mod = 1000000007L;\n        \n        if (n <= 0) {\n            System.out.println(0);\n            return;\n        }\n        \n        long ans = 1;\n        long base = 3;\n        int exp = n - 1;\n        \n        while (exp > 0) {\n            if (exp % 2 == 1) {\n                ans = (ans * base) % mod;\n            }\n            base = (base * base) % mod;\n            exp /= 2;\n        }\n        \n        System.out.println(ans);\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Animal {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);\n        int n = scanner.nextInt();\n        long mod = 1000000007L;\n        \n        if (n <= 0) {\n            System.out.println(0);\n            return;\n        }\n        \n        // The problem asks for the size of maximum matching in the trie of all correct bracket sequences of length 2n\n        // For a trie of bracket sequences, the structure is related to Catalan numbers\n        // The maximum matching in such a trie has a known formula: C(2n, n) / (n+1) * n = Catalan(n) * n\n        // But actually, for the trie of bracket sequences of length 2n, the maximum matching size is C(2n-2, n-1)\n        // This is because the trie has a specific structure where the number of nodes at each level corresponds to ballot numbers\n        \n        // The correct formula for maximum matching in the trie of correct bracket sequences of length 2n is:\n        // C(2n-2, n-1) where C is binomial coefficient\n        \n        // Calculate binomial coefficient C(2n-2, n-1) mod 10^9+7\n        if (n == 1) {\n            System.out.println(1);\n            return;\n        }\n        \n        int N = 2 * n - 2;\n        int K = n - 1;\n        \n        // Precompute factorials and inverse factorials\n        long[] fact = new long[N + 1];\n        long[] invFact = new long[N + 1];\n        \n        fact[0] = 1;\n        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) {\n            fact[i] = (fact[i - 1] * i) % mod;\n        }\n        \n        // Fermat's little theorem for modular inverse since mod is prime\n        invFact[N] = modPow(fact[N], mod - 2, mod);\n        for (int i = N - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n            invFact[i] = (invFact[i + 1] * (i + 1)) % mod;\n        }\n        \n        long result = (fact[N] * invFact[K] % mod) * invFact[N - K] % mod;\n        System.out.println(result);\n    }\n    \n    private static long modPow(long base, long exp, long mod) {\n        long result = 1;\n        base %= mod;\n        while (exp > 0) {\n            if (exp % 2 == 1) {\n                result = (result * base) % mod;\n            }\n            base = (base * base) % mod;\n            exp /= 2;\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-5.960462772236497e-07, -0.00011407678539399058, -1.0728830375228426e-06, 0.0, -8.701899787411094e-05, -3.242440288886428e-05, -0.0004923323867842555, -0.11390721797943115, -2.7418097943154862e-06, -2.7418097943154862e-06, -0.07010950893163681, -7.867782187531702e-06, 0.0, -1.9073468138230965e-06, -6.9141146923357155e-06, -2.3841830625315197e-06, -3.4570634852570947e-06, -7.152555099310121e-07, -1.9073468138230965e-06, -1.7881377516459906e-06, 0.0, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -1.2040065485052764e-05, -1.728519782773219e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -5.960462772236497e-07, -2.455681169521995e-05, -9.536738616588991e-07, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -6.079655122448457e-06, -1.4305